-- run: ./09_fib_recursive.exe
-- measured: 0.1486 s wall clock (min of 3 runs), against 0.1070 s for fib(40) as timed
--       inside the program.
-- note: `-r` is this compiler's `-O2` equivalent (gcc `-O2 -DNDEBUG` plus the
--       compiler's `nochecks` pragma); see 01_branches.nelua.
-- note: the plain double recursion, exactly as specified. fib(40) is 102334155, well
--       below 2^63, so the int64 result is exact.
-- note: measured on this host, built with -r: 0.1070 s. The nominal call count is
--       331160281, which would be 0.32 ns per call — too fast for a real call, and the
--       reason is the C compiler, not Nelua. The generated C really is the double
--       recursion (`return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);`), but gcc 16.1.0 at -O2 partially
--       expands it, and the built function is 267 instructions with one self-call
--       instead of two. The control is a plain C `fib(40)` built by the same gcc with
--       the same flags: 102334155 in 0.1155 s and 0.1188 s. Nelua and C are the same
--       speed here, so this cell measures what the C row measures.
local function fib(n: integer): integer
  if n < 2 then
    return n
  end
  return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)
end

print(fib(40))
